---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
const href = (route: string) => `${base}${route.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Literature · Guided Reading"
  description="Source-faithful guides to foundational and research papers in electronic structure."
  current="reading"
>
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/reading/')}>Guided Reading</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span aria-current="page">Literature</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Literature</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A literature guide helps you read the paper that changed the field without replacing it. The paper’s own problem, sequence, equations, evidence, and limits remain the organizing structure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later clarification is marked as later clarification. For computational papers, guides reconstruct what the authors reported about the physical model, approximations, numerical representation, sampling, and calculated objects; unreported parameters remain unreported.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="density-foundations">
    <h2 id="density-foundations">The density-functional foundations</h2>
    <p>
      The first two papers form the original theorem-to-orbitals sequence. Hohenberg and Kohn establish why the ground-state density can carry an exact variational problem. Kohn and Sham then introduce auxiliary one-electron equations. Levy returns to the density variation itself and defines the universal functional by a constrained search that no longer requires every trial density to be the ground-state density of another potential.
    </p>
    <ol class="section-list source-list">
      <li>
        <h3>Hohenberg & Kohn · Inhomogeneous Electron Gas</h3>
        <p>P. Hohenberg and W. Kohn, <cite>Physical Review</cite> 136, B864–B871 (1964).</p>
        <p class="item-summary">The original density–potential theorem, density variational principle, and the paper’s own nearly-uniform and slowly-varying approximations.</p>
        <a href={href('/reading/literature/hohenberg-kohn-1964/')}>Read the Hohenberg–Kohn guide</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Kohn & Sham · Self-Consistent Equations</h3>
        <p>W. Kohn and L. J. Sham, <cite>Physical Review</cite> 140, A1133–A1138 (1965).</p>
        <p class="item-summary">The route from an unknown density functional to auxiliary orbitals, local exchange–correlation approximations, and a density-dependent self-consistent problem.</p>
        <a href={href('/reading/literature/kohn-sham-1965/')}>Read the Kohn–Sham guide</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Levy · Universal Variational Functionals</h3>
        <p>M. Levy, <cite>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</cite> 76, 6062–6065 (1979).</p>
        <p class="item-summary">The pure-state constrained search, the distinction between <em>N</em>- and <em>v</em>-representability, and parallel searches for restricted states and first-order density matrices.</p>
        <a href={href('/reading/literature/levy-1979/')}>Read the Levy guide</a>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="literature-boundary">
    <h2 id="literature-boundary">Read the source and the Atlas together</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={href('/core/part-iv/')}>Core Part IV · Density Functional Theory</a> gives the Atlas’s modern synthesis. These guides instead preserve what each paper argues in its own order, including historical notation and limitations that a modern textbook often reorganizes.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
